<p>
Irreal readers can&rsquo;t help but know who Álvaro Ramírez is, if for no other reason that I&rsquo;m always blathering on about his <a href="https://xenodium.com/journelly-for-ios">Journelly</a> app that is my most used and useful iOS app. But of course, Ramírez has many other excellent apps, many of which are Emacs packages. In particular, there is his <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/dwim-shell-command">dwim-shell-command</a> that allows you to automate shell commands—particularly those with complex invocations—from the comfort of Emacs. There&rsquo;s also his take on <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/ready-player">music players</a>, <a href="https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell">an uniform AI access app</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/xenodium">a lot more besides</a>.
</p>
<p>
You can check out his <a href="https://xenodium.com/">blog posts</a> or take a look at his <a href="https://xenodium.com/awesome-emacs-on-macos">iOS/macOS/Emacs applications</a>. He&rsquo;s been an independent developer for some time but now he&rsquo;s taken on a new role: <a href="https://xenodium.com/and-then-there-were-three">fatherhood</a>. He&rsquo;s now the proud dad of a new son. He tells us that his output may slow down a bit because, after all, he has to start working on his son&rsquo;s <code>init.el</code>.</p>
